          "name": "What triggers reassessment for Mumbai taxpayers?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "AIS/SFT unreported transactions, CRS foreign income/assets data, investigation wing reports, demonetisation cash deposits, transfer pricing information, survey findings. AO must have 'information' (not suspicion). Patron analyses trigger and develops defence."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is Section 148A and why is it critical?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Post Finance Act 2021: mandatory preliminary inquiry before 148 notice. AO must share information, consider taxpayer's response, pass reasoned order with specified authority approval. First chance to prevent reassessment entirely. Strong 148A response can stop proceedings."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is 'change of opinion' defence?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "If original 143(3) scrutiny examined same issue being reopened, reassessment is 'change of opinion' - not permitted (Supreme Court: CIT vs Kelvinator 2010). AO needs new tangible material. Strongest jurisdictional defence for Mumbai taxpayers with prior scrutiny."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the time limit for reassessment?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Section 149: 3 years from end of AY (normal). Beyond 3 years up to 10 years only if escaped income >= Rs 50 lakh with specified authority approval. Beyond 10 years: not permitted. AO must complete entire 148A procedure within these limits."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I challenge the notice in court?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. If procedural defects (no 148A compliance, no approval, time-barred, change of opinion): writ petition before Bombay High Court to quash. Bombay HC has extensive case law quashing defective notices. Patron coordinates writs with senior advocates."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What should I do if I receive 148A show-cause?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most important response in entire process. Do NOT ignore. Download from portal, understand AO's information, gather counter-evidence, prepare detailed point-by-point response with documentation, submit within deadline. Patron responds within 7 days of receipt."
          }
        },
