    <div class="content-container">
        <div class="text-content">
            <div class="faq-expanded">
                <aside class="faq-expanded__aside">
                    <h2 class="faq-expanded__title">Frequently Asked Questions &ndash; IT Reassessment Section 147 in Mumbai</h2>
                    <p class="faq-expanded__lead">Get answers about reassessment triggers, Section 148A procedure, change of opinion, time limits, court challenge, 148A response strategy, non-response consequences, and reassessment vs scrutiny.</p>
                    @include('partials.faq-enquiry-form', ['enquiryLocation' => 'Mumbai', 'enquiryService' => 'IT Reassessment Section 147 in Mumbai'])
                </aside>
                <div class="faq-expanded__list">
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-1">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What triggers reassessment for Mumbai taxpayers?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>AIS/SFT unreported transactions, CRS foreign income data, investigation wing reports, demonetisation cash deposits, transfer pricing information, survey findings. AO must have 'information' (not suspicion). Patron analyses trigger and develops defence for Mumbai taxpayers.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-2">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is Section 148A and why is it critical?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Post Finance Act 2021: mandatory preliminary inquiry. AO must share information, consider taxpayer's response, pass reasoned order with specified authority approval before 148 notice. First chance to prevent reassessment entirely. Strong 148A response can stop proceedings at this stage.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-3">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is 'change of opinion' defence?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>If original 143(3) scrutiny examined same issue being reopened: 'change of opinion' &ndash; not permitted (Kelvinator SC 2010). AO needs new tangible material. Strongest jurisdictional defence for Mumbai taxpayers whose prior scrutiny examined same transactions.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-4">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is the time limit for reassessment?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Section 149: 3 years from AY end (normal). Beyond 3 years up to 10 years only if escaped income &ge; Rs 50 lakh with PCIT/CCIT approval. Beyond 10 years: not permitted. AO must complete 148A + issue 148 within limits. Patron verifies every Mumbai case.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-5">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Can I challenge the notice in court?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Yes. Writ petition before Bombay High Court if procedural defects: no 148A compliance, no approval, time-barred, change of opinion. Bombay HC has extensive case law quashing defective notices. Patron coordinates writs with senior advocates at Bombay HC.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-6">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What should I do if I receive 148A show-cause?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Most important response in entire process. Do NOT ignore. Download, understand AO's information, gather counter-evidence, prepare detailed point-by-point response with documentation, submit within deadline. Patron analyses and responds within 7 days of receipt for Mumbai taxpayers.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-7">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What happens if I don't respond?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>148A no response: AO proceeds to 148 (non-response noted). 148 no return: best judgement (S144). Queries ignored: additions without input. Non-response at every stage = worst outcome. Patron ensures timely response at every stage for Mumbai taxpayers.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-8">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Reassessment vs regular scrutiny?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Scrutiny (143(3)): first detailed examination of filed return. Reassessment (147): second look triggered by new information about escaped income. Reassessment has additional safeguards (148A, specified authority approval, time limits). Patron represents in both for Mumbai taxpayers.</p></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>


            <!-- Quick Answers -->
            <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-top:32px;">
                <p><strong>Quick Answers</strong></p>
                <p><strong>148A ka show-cause aaya hai &ndash; kya kare?</strong> Sabse important stage! Ignore mat karo. AO ki information carefully padho. Counter-evidence gather karo. Detailed response likho har point pe with proof. Agar strong response diya toh reassessment ruk sakti hai. Patron 7 din mein response submit karta hai.</p>
                <p><strong>Kya purana assessment reopen ho sakta hai?</strong> Haan. 3 saal (normal). 10 saal (agar escaped income Rs 50 lakh+). Finance Act 2021 ke baad 148A mandatory hai. Change of opinion se reopen nahi kar sakte (Kelvinator SC). Patron time limit aur procedure check karta hai.</p>
                <p><strong>Bombay High Court mein challenge kar sakte hain?</strong> Haan. Agar procedural defect hai (148A nahi follow hua, time limit exceed, approval nahi li, change of opinion) toh writ petition file kar sakte ho. Patron Bombay HC mein writ coordinate karta hai senior advocates ke saath.</p>

